> is it correct to assume that these nights are included in part5 because they are being estimated based on other available data even though the night data has been removed from part4?

Yes, that is correct.

If you feel that estimates are concerningly wrong, consider parameter data_cleaning_file which allows you to specific which window should be excluded from part 5.

Another option is to work with the full daysummary csv report from part 5 as stored in the results/QC folder. Here, remove the nights you do not want to work with and then aggregate this to person level outside GGIR.

Best wishes,

Vincent

On Tuesday, 29 October 2024 at 6:30 PM, Kelsey Sewell <kelsey...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Hi Vincent,
>
> Thank you so much for taking the time to reply.
>
> While we referenced CoDA, our question is specifically about why nights removed from the part4 metadata continue to appear in the part5 output. Based on our reading and interpretation of cleaning code 5, is it correct to assume that these nights are included in part5 because they are being estimated based on other available data even though the night data has been removed from part4?
>
> Best wishes,
> Kelsey & Audrey
>
> On Monday, October 28, 2024 at 6:00:13 AM UTC-4 Vincent van Hees wrote:
> > Dear Kelsey,
> >
> > Based on correspondence with other researchers I assumed that for sleep research it is critical to split diary guided analyses from non-diary guided analysis as we do in the GGIR part 4 output, while for compositional analysis any estimate of the night is acceptable because:
> > Sleep is only one of several components, by which a bit more uncertainty is considered acceptable.
> > If we only look at diary-guided sleep the drop in sample size might be too high given that we need complete data for two consecutive nights to describe a single day.
> >
